==========================================
DAY 22 NOTES — System Monitoring (top, htop, vmstat)
Date: 20-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- top gives a LIVE, refreshing view of processes and system stats (vs ps aux's one-time snapshot)
- Load average = how many processes want CPU time, averaged over 1/5/15 minutes
- Load average only makes sense relative to CPU core count (check with nproc)
- top keys: M = sort by memory, P = sort by CPU, q = quit, k = kill a process
- htop is a friendlier, color-coded version of top (needs: sudo apt install htop)
- vmstat shows system-wide memory/CPU/IO stats over a time window, non-interactive
- top -bn1 gives a ONE-TIME snapshot — useful in scripts where live refresh doesn't work

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] top (navigating, sorting with M/P)
[ ] htop (installing and using)
[ ] vmstat 1 5
[ ] nproc / lscpu
[ ] top -bn1 (non-interactive snapshot)

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ top (note load average and top process)
